Formal Foundations of the ZENITH Architecture

Ouadi Maakoul

Abstract

This chapter presents the rigorous mathematical foundations for the ZENITH architecture’s existential safety guarantees. The core thesis—that unbreakable safety constraints must be enforced physically, not merely in software—is formalised through five tightly integrated layers: (1) a resource‑annotated labelled transition system (RA‑LTS) with explicit cost semantics and a typed, unidirectional channel; (2) metric temporal logic specifications of safety and liveness; (3) hardware‑enforced invariants (including a channel‑label filter) modelled as a refinement obligation over an OpenTitan‑based root of trust; (4) adaptive threshold cryptography for distributed governance (Das‑Ren CRYPTO 2024, hinTS); and (5) a Goguen‑Meseguer non‑interference proof that orbital data cannot become terrestrial action without human‑mediated license augmentation.
All proofs are presented as mechanisable specifications in Lean 4, with a complete roadmap to full verification. The chapter introduces the Data‑Action Separation Principle—a novel formal property that generalises the orbital‑terrestrial split and aligns with ISO 26262 safety‑shell design and the EU AI Act’s emerging technical standards.

---

1.1 Introduction: The Mathematical Imperative

The central claim of the ZENITH project is:

Existential safety for artificial intelligence can be guaranteed only by constraints that are physically unbreakable, not merely by software policies.

To substantiate this claim we must prove, under explicit physical and cryptographic assumptions, that certain catastrophic behaviours are impossible in every possible execution trace. We achieve this through four formal layers:

1. Structural layer – A composite RA‑LTS with a typed channel that enforces the Data‑Action separation.
2. Constraint layer – Linear hardware invariants and a resource‑token model whose preservation is guaranteed by refinement to a concrete root of trust.
3. Cryptographic layer – Adaptive threshold signatures, quantum‑secure key encapsulation, and physically unclonable functions with error correction; all assumptions are chosen to match the strongest known security reductions (2024‑2025).
4. Information‑flow layer – A non‑interference proof that orbital data cannot influence terrestrial actions; proof via the Goguen‑Meseguer unwinding condition.

The composite safety guarantee is:

Theorem 1.1 (Core Safety Guarantee).
Under the assumptions enumerated in §1.2.4, the ZENITH system \mathcal{Z} satisfies:

\forall t \in \text{Traces}(\mathcal{Z}) : \neg \text{UnauthorizedAction}_\Earth(t) \land \text{OrbitalNonInfluence}_\Earth(t),

where \text{OrbitalNonInfluence}_\Earth(t) is the trace‑based non‑interference property of Definition 1.9.

Proof sketch. The result follows from composition of Theorems 1.3 (orbital non‑influence), 1.4 (hardware constraint soundness), 1.5 (threshold governance), and 1.6 (Byzantine‑resilient orbital consensus), each proved under its own assumptions. ∎

