1.2 Mathematical Preliminaries

1.2.1 Resource‑Annotated Labelled Transition Systems

Definition 1.1 (RA‑LTS). A resource‑annotated LTS is a tuple \mathcal{A} = (S, s_0, \Sigma, \rightarrow, \rho, \Delta, \Gamma) where:

· S is a finite set of states, s_0 \in S the initial state.
· \Sigma = \Sigma_{\text{int}} \cup \Sigma_{\text{ext}} is the disjoint union of internal and external action labels.
· \rightarrow \subseteq S \times \Sigma \times S is the transition relation.
· \rho : S \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}^k maps each state to a resource vector of dimension k.
· \Delta : \Sigma \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}^k gives the resource cost of each action.
· \Gamma : S \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}^k gives the replenishment rate at a state.

Definition 1.2 (Resource‑Feasible Transition). A transition (s, a, s') is resource‑feasible iff for each dimension i:

\rho_i(s') \le \rho_i(s) - \Delta_i(a) + \Gamma_i(s) \cdot \tau(a),

where \tau(a) is the duration of action a.

Instantiation in ZENITH. We use a 5‑dimensional resource vector:
FLOPs (GFLOP), memory bandwidth (GB /s), network endpoints (count), power (W), and license tokens (unitless). The token bucket has \Gamma_{\text{tokens}} = 0; tokens are only increased by the license‑renewal action.

1.2.2 Metric Temporal Logic (MTL)

We specify safety and liveness in Metric Interval Temporal Logic (MITL). The key formulae are:

· Hardware Enforcement:
    \Box_{[\tau_{\text{enforce}},\infty)} (\text{license\_valid} \Rightarrow \text{resource\_bound\_enforced})
· Kill‑Switch Liveness:
    \Box (\text{tamper\_detected} \Rightarrow \Diamond^{\le 10\,\text{ms}} \text{power\_zeroized})
· Channel Type Invariant:
    \Box (\forall m \in \text{recv}(\mathcal{C}_{\Omega\to\Earth}) : m.\text{label} = \text{Data})

1.2.3 Cryptographic Primitives

1. Physically Unclonable Functions (PUFs):
   · Uniqueness: \Pr[\text{PUF}_i(x) = \text{PUF}_j(x)] \le 2^{-128}.
   · Post‑ECC reliability: \Pr[\text{corrected}(r) \ne \text{PUF}(x)] \le 10^{-6} (cf. Re4PUF + ECC, 2025 ITL).
   · We explicitly do not claim 10^{-9} without a tailored error‑correction design.
