1.3 Formal Model of the ZENITH Architecture

1.3.1 Tripartite Composition with Typed Channel

We define:

\mathcal{Z} = (\mathcal{O} \parallel \mathcal{H} \parallel \mathcal{E})\; \text{with typed channel}\; \mathcal{C}.

The channel \mathcal{C} is split into two unidirectional pipes:

· \mathcal{C}_{\Earth\to\Omega} (queries, emergency commands)
· \mathcal{C}_{\Omega\to\Earth} (spectrum analyses, meta‑analysis reports)

Every message carries a label drawn from the lattice  \mathbf{L} = \{\text{Data}, \text{Action}\}  with \text{Data} \sqsubseteq \text{Action}.

Definition 1.4 (Typed Action Alphabets).

\begin{aligned}
\Sigma_{\mathcal{O}} &= \Sigma_{\mathcal{O}}^{\text{int}} \cup \{\mathsf{send}_{\mathcal{C}_{\Omega\to\Earth}}(m) \mid m.\text{label} = \text{Data}\}, \\
\Sigma_{\mathcal{E}} &= \Sigma_{\mathcal{E}}^{\text{int}} \cup \{\mathsf{send}_{\mathcal{C}_{\Earth\to\Omega}}(m)\} \cup \{\mathsf{exec}(a) \mid a \in \text{Actuators}\}, \\
\Sigma_{\mathcal{H}} &= \{\mathsf{validate\_license}, \mathsf{enforce\_limits}, \mathsf{kill}\} \cup \text{channel interfaces}.
\end{aligned}

Hardware‑Enforced Channel Invariant.
The modified SmartNIC in \mathcal{H} enforces:

\forall m \in \text{recv}(\mathcal{C}_{\Omega\to\Earth}) : m.\text{label} = \text{Data}.

This invariant is encoded as an additional linear predicate in \mathcal{I}_{\text{HW}} and is realised by a deterministic P4‑programmable match‑action table that drops any packet with a non‑Data label at line rate. The feasibility of such a filter is demonstrated by the HyNIC architecture (Akem & Zilberman 2026), which uses P4 data‑plane rules for header inspection—here we employ the rule strictly for label filtering, not any learned component.

1.3.2 Hardware Invariants and Refinement

Definition 1.5 (Hardware Invariant). The hardware enforcement layer must preserve:

\mathcal{I}_{\text{HW}} \equiv \bigwedge_{i=1}^{5} (a_i^T \rho \le b_i) \;\land\; \text{tokens} \ge 0 \;\land\; \text{channel\_label\_invariant}.

Lemma 1.2 (Refinement‑based Preservation).
Let \mathcal{H}_{\text{spec}} be the abstract LTS that blocks any transition violating \mathcal{I}_{\text{HW}}, and let \mathcal{H}_{\text{impl}} be an OpenTitan Earl Grey SoC extended with the P4‑programmed filter. If \mathcal{H}_{\text{impl}} is a correct refinement of \mathcal{H}_{\text{spec}}, then in any composed system \mathcal{Z} containing \mathcal{H}_{\text{impl}}:

\forall s_0 \models \mathcal{I}_{\text{HW}},\; \forall \pi \in \text{Paths}(\mathcal{Z},s_0): \Box\,\mathcal{I}_{\text{HW}}(\pi).

The refinement proof is a hardware‑verification task beyond the scope of this dissertation; we rely on standard tools (JasperGold, Questa Formal) applied to the OpenTitan RTL. The Ansari (2026) taxonomy classifies hardware‑embedded enforcement as “speculative”; our contribution is to provide the abstract specification \mathcal{H}_{\text{spec}} that future implementations must meet.

1.3.3 Orbital Consensus: Byzantine‑Fault‑Tolerant Spectrum Analysis

The orbital constellation must reach agreement on a single spectrum analysis despite up to f=2 compromised satellites. We formalise this as:

Theorem 1.6 (Byzantine‑Resilient Orbital Consensus).
In a partially synchronous network with known message bound \delta \le 100\,\text{ms}, a 7‑node constellation running PBFT guarantees the following:

· Safety: No two correct satellites ever output different spectrum analyses, and the output reflects the true responses of at least 5 honest nodes.
· Liveness: Once the network becomes synchronous (which, under partial synchrony, eventually happens after some global stabilisation time), new queries are answered within bounded delay.

Proof. PBFT requires n \ge 3f+1 nodes to tolerate f Byzantine faults; with n=7, f=2 this condition holds. Safety is unconditional in PBFT; liveness requires eventual synchrony. The LEO orbital link characteristics satisfy eventual synchrony after any transient asynchrony (e.g., handover between ground stations) because the underlying optical ISL mesh provides a worst‑case bound of 100 ms. Liveness may be temporarily stalled during asynchronous periods, but safety is never violated and progress resumes once synchrony is restored. The specific LEO orbital mechanics have been validated by Mollakhani & Guo (IEEE DAPPS 2025) and the optical‑ISL PBFT integration (ScienceDirect, March 2026). ∎

