1.4 Safety and Liveness Proofs

1.4.1 Orbital Non‑Influence — Unwinding Proof

Definition 1.9 (Orbital Non‑Influence).
Let \text{trace}_{\Earth}(\pi) be the projection of trace \pi onto terrestrial exec actions. The system satisfies orbital non‑influence if for any two traces \pi_1, \pi_2 that are identical except possibly for the payloads of messages on \mathcal{C}_{\Omega\to\Earth}, we have \text{trace}_{\Earth}(\pi_1) = \text{trace}_{\Earth}(\pi_2).

Theorem 1.3 (Orbital Non‑Influence).
Under assumptions A1, A6, and A7 (where A7 depends on A6), the ZENITH architecture satisfies orbital non‑influence.

Proof. We apply the Goguen‑Meseguer unwinding technique (Rushby’s formalisation). Define an equivalence relation \sim on system states:

s_1 \sim s_2 \quad\text{iff}\quad
\begin{cases}
\text{The terrestrial state components (including tokens, resource vectors) are identical}, \\
\text{The pending orbital messages differ only in Data payloads}.
\end{cases}

We prove the three unwinding conditions:

1. Output consistency: If s_1 \sim s_2 and a terrestrial exec(a) action occurs from s_1, then the same action occurs from s_2 and the resulting states are still \sim. This holds because exec(a) is enabled only by Action‑labelled messages from \mathcal{H}, which depend solely on license tokens and resource usage—both identical by \sim.
2. Low step preservation: Any terrestrial action (including license renewal) that does not depend on orbital Data payloads preserves \sim. This is immediate from the fact that the terrestrial transition guards are functions of the terrestrial state and Action‑labelled messages from \mathcal{H}, never of Data payloads.
3. High step equivalence: Receipt of an orbital Data message leads to states that are still \sim, because the message payload only updates a “last orbital report” buffer that is never read by any Action‑enabling guard. The hardware label‑filter invariant (A7, which relies on A6) guarantees that no Action‑labelled message can ever arrive from Orbit; thus orbital steps cannot introduce Action triggers.

By induction over the length of traces, any two traces differing only in orbital Data produce the same sequence of terrestrial exec actions. ∎

1.4.2 Hardware Enforcement: Constraint Soundness

Theorem 1.4 (Constraint Soundness — Refinement‑based).
Assume A6 (refinement correctness). Then no software execution can violate the licensed resource bounds:

\Pr[\,\exists t: \rho(t) \not\models \mathcal{I}_{\text{HW}} \mid \text{refinement\_correct}\,] = 0.

Proof. As in the original sketch, but now the condition is the refinement relation, not an unexamined “hardware correct” statement. Physical tampering that bypasses the circuits triggers the kill‑switch (§1.5.2). ∎

1.4.3 Distributed Governance: Adaptive Threshold Security

Theorem 1.5 (No Unilateral Control — Adaptive Adversary).
Under A4 (DDH + co‑CDH in ROM), no adversary controlling fewer than 5 NASAg regional centers can issue, modify, or revoke an ME‑class license, even if centers are corrupted adaptively during the protocol.

Proof. The license issuance protocol instantiates the Das‑Ren (CRYPTO 2024) adaptively secure (5,7)‑threshold BLS scheme. The main theorem of that paper (Theorem 2) guarantees existential unforgeability against adaptive adversaries in the Random Oracle Model. Proactive security (key refresh) is added via hinTS (ePrint 2023/567) to guard against mobile adversaries. ∎

