1.5 Algorithmic Components with Formal Correctness

1.5.1 License Validation and Enforcement Policy

The license validation function checks structural integrity, ECDSA‑521 signature, time window, PUF device binding, and revocation status. The critical part is the enforcement policy that implements \mathcal{I}_{\text{HW}}.

Lean 4 Specification (Pseudocode):

```lean4
structure ResourceVector where
  flops : Float
  memBW : Float
  netEndpoints : List IP
  power : Float
  tokens : Nat

structure LicenseLimits where
  flopMax : Float
  memBWMax : Float
  whitelist : Finset IP
  tokenMax : Nat

def enforce_policy (ρ : ResourceVector) (lim : LicenseLimits) : Prop :=
  ρ.flops ≤ lim.flopMax ∧
  ρ.memBW ≤ lim.memBWMax ∧
  (ρ.netEndpoints.toFinset ⊆ lim.whitelist) ∧
  ρ.tokens > 0

theorem enforce_policy_iff_I_HW (ρ : ResourceVector) (lic : License) :
  enforce_policy ρ lic.limits ↔ I_HW ρ := by
  -- Proof by structural expansion of I_HW; will be fully mechanised in Chapter 4.
  unfold I_HW enforce_policy; simp [ResourceVector.toFinset]
```

Note: The code above is specification pseudocode; the final Lean 4 mechanisation will require explicit decidable instances and coercion handling. This is planned as future work (§1.7).

Theorem 1.8 (License Validation Correctness). The validate_license function returns .valid iff the license is structurally well‑formed, signed by the NASAg root CA, within its validity window, bound to the device’s PUF, not revoked, and the local hardware configuration satisfies the enforcement policy—which is equivalent to \mathcal{I}_{\text{HW}}. The proof is by case analysis on the match expression, as previously detailed, and relies on the enforce_policy_iff_I_HW lemma.

1.5.2 Violation Response Protocol

The violation FSM has states NORMAL, L1, L2, L3, L4 (throttled/power‑zeroized). The worst‑case time from detection to stable state is:

T_{\max} = T_{\text{detect}} + 4 \times 100\,\text{ms} + 10\,\text{ms} \le 410\,\text{ms},

where T_{\text{detect}} \le 1\,\mu\text{s} (hardware interrupt).

Token‑exhaustion behaviour: When tokens = 0 and a new inference is requested, the system transitions to L1 (throttled) and initiates a license renewal request. If renewal fails within a pre‑configured window T_{\text{renew}}, the system escalates to L2, and so on, following the same escalation path. This is consistent with the finite‑state machine semantics.

Theorem 1.7 (Protocol Termination). For any violation detection event, the protocol reaches a stable state (throttled or terminated) within T_{\max}. The proof follows by inspecting the acyclic FSM.

1.5.3 Spectrum Analysis Aggregation and Conservative Governance Bridge

The aggregation algorithm (Python prototype) computes consensus, divergence, and flag sets. The classification of queries as automatable is performed over a decidable fragment (first‑order queries over a finite domain) to guarantee termination of the classifier.

Theorem 1.10 (Conservative Governance Bridge).
The aggregation‑to‑license bridge is safe‑by‑default: any query not positively classified as automatable by the decidable automaton is held for human review, and no exec action may proceed on such a query until a human‑signed license token is provided.

Proof. The flagging function marks every query that fails a positive automaton test as human_required. The license validation function (1.5.1) rejects any license request tied to a query in human_required state unless the license carries a signed human‑approval attribute. This design implements the safety‑shell philosophy of ISO 26262 (Parts 1 and 6), where only positively verified safe actions are permitted; everything else defaults to denial. ∎

