1.9 The Data‑Action Separation Principle

The formal development of this chapter crystallises a general principle for existential AI safety:

Principle (Data‑Action Separation).
In any AI system whose unrestricted execution could cause unacceptable harm, the epistemic outputs (data, analysis, prediction) must be type‑theoretically separated from executive outputs (actions, commands, actuations) by a physically‑enforced type lattice. No data may be promoted to an action without an explicit, human‑mediated authorisation token that is checked by a trusted hardware layer.

Formally, a system satisfies the Data‑Action Separation Principle (DASP) if there exists a mapping \varphi from its message types to the lattice \mathbf{L} = \{\text{Data}, \text{Action}\} that is preserved by all channel transitions, and no channel admits a message with \varphi(m) = \text{Action} unless that message carries a valid, human‑signed authorisation token. The ZENITH architecture is one concrete realisation; any architecture meeting this principle would, by an analogous unwinding proof, enjoy the same orbital‑style non‑influence property. This principle can serve as a checkable design criterion for future safety‑critical AI systems.

