A.1 Sets, Functions, and Sequences

· \mathbb{N} denotes the natural numbers \{0,1,2,\dots\}.
· \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} is the set of non‑negative real numbers.
· For a set X, we write \mathcal{P}(X) for the power set of X.
· A finite sequence (or trace) over an alphabet \Sigma is written as \langle a_1, a_2, \dots, a_n\rangle or simply a_1 a_2 \cdots a_n.
    \Sigma^* denotes the set of all finite sequences over \Sigma.
· The projection of a trace onto a subset A \subseteq \Sigma is the subsequence consisting of those actions in A, preserving order.

---

A.2 Labelled Transition Systems (LTS)

Definition A.2.1 (LTS). A labelled transition system is a tuple (S, s_0, \Sigma, \rightarrow) where

· S is a set of states,
· s_0 \in S is the initial state,
· \Sigma is a set of action labels,
· \rightarrow \subseteq S \times \Sigma \times S is the transition relation.

We write s \xrightarrow{a} s' for (s,a,s') \in \rightarrow.

Definition A.2.2 (Trace, Path). An execution fragment is a finite or infinite alternating sequence s_0 a_0 s_1 a_1 s_2 \dots such that s_i \xrightarrow{a_i} s_{i+1} for every step. A trace is the projection of an execution fragment onto the sequence of action labels. The set of all traces of \mathcal{A} starting from s_0 is denoted \text{Traces}(\mathcal{A}).

---

A.3 Resource‑Annotated LTS (RA‑LTS)

Definition A.3.1 (RA‑LTS). A resource‑annotated labelled transition system is a tuple \mathcal{A} = (S, s_0, \Sigma, \rightarrow, \rho, \Delta, \Gamma) where

· (S, s_0, \Sigma, \rightarrow) is an LTS,
· \rho : S \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}^k assigns to each state a k-dimensional resource vector,
· \Delta : \Sigma \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}^k gives the resource cost of each action,
· \Gamma : S \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}^k gives the resource replenishment rate per time unit in state s.

A transition (s,a,s') is resource‑feasible iff, for each dimension i,

\rho_i(s') \le \rho_i(s) - \Delta_i(a) + \Gamma_i(s) \cdot \tau(a),

where \tau(a) is the duration of action a.

Instantiation for ZENITH. We fix k=5 with the following dimensions:

1. FLOPs (in GFLOP)
2. memory bandwidth (in GB /s)
3. number of distinct network endpoints in use
4. power (in W)
5. license tokens (unitless, representing permission to execute)

For license tokens, we set \Gamma_{\text{tokens}}(s)=0 for all s; tokens can only be increased by a dedicated license_renewal action.

---

A.4 Metric Interval Temporal Logic (MITL)

We use a simple metric temporal logic over discrete time. Let \mathcal{P} be a set of atomic propositions. The syntax includes:

\phi ::= p \mid \neg\phi \mid \phi_1 \land \phi_2 \mid \bigcirc \phi \mid \phi_1 \mathbin{\mathcal{U}} \phi_2 \mid \Box_{[a,b]} \phi \mid \Diamond_{[a,b]}\phi

where a,b \in \mathbb{N} with a\le b. The semantics follows the usual definitions for infinite sequences of states indexed by natural numbers (discrete time steps). \Box_{[a,b]}\phi holds at time t iff \phi holds at every time t' with t+a \le t' \le t+b. \Diamond_{[a,b]}\phi is the dual.

---

A.5 Cryptographic Definitions

We give a bare‑bones summary; full formal definitions appear in standard references.

Digital Signatures. A signature scheme consists of algorithms (\mathsf{KeyGen},\mathsf{Sign},\mathsf{Verify}) such that for any valid key pair (pk,sk) and message m, \mathsf{Verify}(pk, m, \mathsf{Sign}(sk,m)) = 1. Security is expressed as existential unforgeability under chosen‑message attack (EUF‑CMA).

Threshold Signatures. A (t,n)-threshold signature scheme distributes signing capability among n parties. Any subset of at least t parties can produce a valid signature; any subset of fewer than t learns nothing and cannot forge. We use the Das‑Ren (CRYPTO 2024) adaptively secure threshold BLS scheme, based on pairing‑friendly curves (BLS12‑381) and proven secure under the Decisional Diffie‑Hellman (DDH) and computational co‑Diffie‑Hellman (co‑CDH) assumptions in the Random Oracle Model.

Physically Unclonable Functions (PUFs). A PUF is a physical entity that, when given a challenge x \in \{0,1\}^n, produces a response y \in \{0,1\}^m that is unique to that device and hard to predict. We assume a family of PUFs with:

· Uniqueness: \Pr[PUF_i(x) = PUF_j(x)] \le 2^{-128} for i \ne j,
· Reliability after error correction: \Pr[\text{corrected}(r) \ne PUF(x)] \le 10^{-6}.

Post‑Quantum Cryptography. We rely on Kyber‑1024 (IND‑CCA2 KEM) and Dilithium‑5 (EUF‑CMA signature), both standardised by NIST and believed secure against quantum adversaries under lattice assumptions.

---

A.6 Byzantine Fault Tolerance (PBFT)

We recall the standard Practical Byzantine Fault Tolerance (PBFT) protocol for a network of n nodes of which at most f are Byzantine. PBFT guarantees:

· Safety: No two correct nodes ever decide on conflicting values, provided n \ge 3f+1. Safety holds regardless of synchrony.
· Liveness: Once the network becomes synchronous and a leader timeout expires, a new value is committed within a bounded number of message steps.

We assume a partially synchronous model: after some unknown Global Stabilisation Time (GST), all messages between correct nodes arrive within a known bound \delta. In our LEO setting, the known bound is \delta = 100\,\text{ms}.

