where \Sigma_{\mathcal{H}} includes validate_license, enforce_limits, kill, and channel‑interface actions. \mathcal{H} maintains the invariant \mathcal{I}_{\text{HW}} as part of its state condition. Any attempt by \mathcal{E} to perform a transition that would violate \mathcal{I}_{\text{HW}} is either blocked or results in a throttling action.

---

B.2 Typed Channel \mathcal{C}

The channel is a pair of unidirectional pipes enforced by the hardware filter.

Messages. A message is a tuple (\text{label}, \text{payload}) where \text{label} \in \{\text{Data}, \text{Action}\} and payload is the content (query, report, etc.).

Sending Rule for \mathcal{C}_{\Omega\to\Earth}: Only \mathcal{O} may send on this channel, and only messages with label = Data.

Receiving Rule for \mathcal{C}_{\Omega\to\Earth}: The hardware layer \mathcal{H} inspects every incoming message. If label ≠ Data, the message is dropped; otherwise it is forwarded to \mathcal{E} as an input with label Data.

The channel is physically realised by a deterministic P4‑programmed match‑action table in a SmartNIC at the Earth ground station. The rule is:

```
if (packet.label != Data) { drop; }
else { forward; }
```

No message with label = Action can ever appear in \mathcal{E}’s input buffer from Orbit.

---

B.3 Hardware Invariant \mathcal{I}_{\text{HW}}

We define a predicate over the terrestrial state s (which includes \rho, tokens, and the channel buffers):

\mathcal{I}_{\text{HW}}(s) \equiv 
\begin{cases}
\rho_1 \le L_1 \land \cdots \land \rho_5 \le L_5 & \text{(resource caps)} \\
\text{tokens} \ge 0 & \text{(non‑negative tokens)} \\
\forall m \in \text{recv\_buf}(\mathcal{C}_{\Omega\to\Earth}) : m.\text{label} = \text{Data} & \text{(channel label invariant)}
\end{cases}

The abstract hardware specification \mathcal{H}_{\text{spec}} is an LTS with the same actions as \mathcal{H} but with the property that any transition (s,a,s') is allowed only if \mathcal{I}_{\text{HW}}(s') holds; if a transition would produce a state violating the invariant, it is replaced by a throttling action that keeps the invariant true (e.g., by reducing resource usage or rejecting the request).

---

B.4 Refinement Relation

We say that a concrete implementation \mathcal{H}_{\text{impl}} refines \mathcal{H}_{\text{spec}} (written \mathcal{H}_{\text{impl}} \sqsubseteq \mathcal{H}_{\text{spec}}) if every trace of \mathcal{H}_{\text{impl}} is also a trace of \mathcal{H}_{\text{spec}}. This is the standard notion of trace refinement.

Assumption A6 asserts that such a refinement relation exists and is proved via hardware verification tools. If the refinement holds, then any safety property satisfied by all traces of \mathcal{H}_{\text{spec}} is also satisfied by all traces of \mathcal{H}_{\text{impl}}.

