C.2 Proof of Theorem 1.3 (Orbital Non‑Influence)

Statement. Under A1, A6, A7 (with A7 contingent on A6), the ZENITH architecture satisfies orbital non‑influence: for any two traces \pi_1, \pi_2 that differ only in the payloads of orbital Data messages (but agree on all Action labels and all Earth‑side transitions), the sequence of terrestrial exec actions is identical.

Proof. We employ the Goguen‑Meseguer unwinding technique. Define an equivalence relation \sim on the composite state of \mathcal{Z}.

State space. The state of \mathcal{Z} can be written as S_{\mathcal{O}} \times S_{\mathcal{H}} \times S_{\mathcal{E}} augmented with channel buffers. We focus on the part that affects terrestrial exec actions:

· the terrestrial state component s_\mathcal{E} (including token count, resource vector, license validity flags),
· the content of the input buffer from \mathcal{C}_{\Omega\to\Earth}, which we separate into label (always Data) and payload.

Define s \sim s' iff:

· they agree on the terrestrial state: s_\mathcal{E} = s'_\mathcal{E},
· they agree on the identification of all pending Action‑labelled messages from \mathcal{H},
· any orbital Data messages present may have different payloads, but their labels are both Data.

We now verify the three classic unwinding conditions (Rushby 1992).

Condition 1 – Output consistency: Suppose s_1 \sim s_2 and a terrestrial exec(a) action occurs from s_1. The transition exec(a) is possible only if there is an Action‑labelled token available, and the resource check passes. By s_1 \sim s_2, the terrestrial state (tokens, resources) is identical, and the set of Action messages is the same. Therefore the guard is satisfied in s_2 as well, and the same exec(a) can fire from s_2. After the transition, tokens are decremented identically, so the new states again satisfy \sim. (No orbital Data message is consumed, so their differing payloads are irrelevant.)

Condition 2 – Low step preservation (terrestrial actions): Consider any transition s \xrightarrow{a} s' where a is not a receipt of an orbital Data message. This includes internal Earth actions, license renewal, etc. By definition, such transitions depend only on the terrestrial state and on Action‑labelled messages; they do not examine orbital Data payloads. Therefore, if s_1 \sim s_2, the same transition is enabled and leads to states s_1' \sim s_2'.

Condition 3 – High step equivalence (receiving orbital Data): Now consider a transition that consumes an orbital Data message m from \mathcal{C}_{\Omega\to\Earth}. By the hardware invariant (A7) enforced by \mathcal{H}, m.\text{label} = \text{Data}. The receipt updates the “last orbital report” buffer inside \mathcal{E}, but this buffer is never read by any guard that enables an exec(a) action. The buffer is only used for human‑mediated analysis and license decisions, which occur via separate Action messages from \mathcal{H} that are independent of the orbital payload. Thus after the step, the two states differ only in the new payload (and the buffer is not part of the terrestrial decision state), so \sim holds. Crucially, the set of pending Action messages and the terrestrial token/resources are unchanged.

Induction. Given any two full traces \pi_1, \pi_2 that start from \sim-equivalent initial states (which hold trivially, as all buffers empty) and follow the same sequence of actions except that at some steps the orbital Data payloads differ, we can prove by induction on the length of the trace that after each step the states remain \sim-equivalent and the terrestrial projection of exec actions is identical. The three conditions guarantee that the equivalence is preserved and that exec actions are triggered exactly when they would be in the other trace.

Therefore, orbital non‑influence holds. ∎

