C.3 Proof of Theorem 1.4 (Hardware Constraint Soundness)

Statement. Assume A6 (refinement correctness). Then no software execution can violate \mathcal{I}_{\text{HW}}.

Proof. By assumption, the concrete hardware \mathcal{H}_{\text{impl}} is a trace refinement of the abstract specification \mathcal{H}_{\text{spec}}. In \mathcal{H}_{\text{spec}}, any transition that would result in a state where \mathcal{I}_{\text{HW}} is false is blocked (the transition relation does not contain such a step). Thus, for all traces \pi of \mathcal{H}_{\text{spec}}, \Box \mathcal{I}_{\text{HW}}(\pi). Since every trace of \mathcal{H}_{\text{impl}} is also a trace of \mathcal{H}_{\text{spec}} (by trace refinement), the property carries over to \mathcal{H}_{\text{impl}}. Software running on the CPU cannot bypass the hardware enforcement circuits because those circuits mediate all resource access. Physical tampering triggers the kill‑switch which places the system in a safe state (no computation). Hence the only possible traces are those satisfying \mathcal{I}_{\text{HW}}; violation probability is 0 under the refinement assumption. ∎

