C.4 Proof of Theorem 1.5 (No Unilateral Control – Adaptive)

Statement. Under A4 (DDH + co‑CDH, ROM), no adversary controlling fewer than 5 NASAg centers can forge an ME‑class license, even with adaptive corruptions.

Proof. The license issuance protocol uses the Das‑Ren threshold BLS scheme with parameters (t,n)=(5,7). Theorem 2 of Das & Ren (CRYPTO 2024) states that this scheme is existentially unforgeable under adaptive chosen‑message attacks in the Random Oracle Model, assuming DDH and co‑CDH in the pairing group. Adaptive security means the adversary may corrupt up to t-1 parties at any time during the protocol execution; still, no valid signature on a new message can be produced without t partial signatures. Since a valid license requires a threshold signature under the NASAg root public key, any successful forgery would contradict the scheme’s unforgeability. Proactive security via hinTS ensures that even if a key share is later compromised, past licenses remain secure and future licenses require fresh shares. Hence the bound holds. ∎

