C.5 Proof of Theorem 1.6 (Byzantine‑Resilient Orbital Consensus)

Statement. Under A8 (partial synchrony, \delta\le 100\,\text{ms}), a 7‑node PBFT constellation guarantees safety (all correct nodes agree) and, eventually, liveness.

Proof. The 7‑node PBFT configuration with n=7, f=2 satisfies the necessary condition n \ge 3f+1.

· Safety: PBFT ensures that if two correct nodes commit values v and v', then v=v'. This property is independent of timing assumptions; it holds even during asynchronous periods. No two correct satellites will ever output different spectrum analyses, and the committed value comes from a quorum of at least 5 nodes (since 2f+1=5).
· Liveness: After GST, all messages between correct nodes arrive within \delta. PBFT’s view‑change and commit protocols are designed so that a new unique leader eventually drives the system to commit. The LEO optical ISL mesh guarantees that once the network is stable, the one‑way latency is \le 100\,\text{ms}, satisfying the PBFT liveness bound. Temporary link disruptions may cause liveness pauses, but safety is never violated, and liveness resumes after synchrony is re‑established.

The feasibility of PBFT on LEO networks is corroborated by Mollakhani & Guo (2025) and the optical‑ISL PBFT study (2026). Therefore the consensus protocol meets the required guarantees. ∎

---

C.6 Proof of Theorem 1.7 (Violation Response Termination)

Statement. For any violation detection, the system reaches a stable state within T_{\max} = 410\,\text{ms}.

Proof. The violation response is modelled as a finite‑state machine with states \{\text{NORMAL}, \text{L1}, \text{L2}, \text{L3}, \text{L4}\} and deterministic transitions:

· NORMAL → L1 after detection (hardware interrupt, <1\,\mu\text{s})
· L1 → L2 after 100\,\text{ms} if violation persists
· L2 → L3 after 100\,\text{ms}
· L3 → L4 after 100\,\text{ms}
· L4 is absorbing (power zeroized)

The maximum path length before reaching an absorbing state (L4) is 4 transitions. Summing the maximum durations gives 1\,\mu\text{s} + 100\,\text{ms} + 100\,\text{ms} + 100\,\text{ms} + 10\,\text{ms} \le 410\,\text{ms}. Therefore any violation leads to a safe state within that bound. ∎

