C.8 Proof of Theorem 1.10 (Conservative Governance Bridge)

Statement. The bridge from spectrum analysis to license validation is safe‑by‑default: any query not positively classified as automatable by the decidable automaton is held for human review, and no exec action can proceed until a human‑signed token is provided.

Proof. The classification automaton M is a deterministic finite‑state machine that recognises a decidable set of query patterns L_{\text{safe}}. For a query q, if q \in L_{\text{safe}}, no flag is raised; otherwise q is marked human_required. The license validation function (Theorem 1.8) refuses to issue a license token for any action associated with a human_required query unless the license also contains a valid signature from a human‑authorisation sub‑key. By the properties of the threshold signature scheme, such a signature can only be produced with explicit human involvement (a separate signing ceremony). Thus any attempt to execute an exec(a) for an unclassified query will find no token and be blocked, defaulting to a safe denial. The design is a direct implementation of the safety‑shell principle of ISO 26262, where every action is forbidden unless positively allowed. ∎

